$ grep -n "RemoteSkipped { .. } => continue" src/am/ec_distann/custom_scan.rs
1318:            CustomScanOutputRow::RemoteSkipped { .. } => continue,

Result: three distinct existing visibility outcomes, not one.

  - `Local(tid)`   (user heap)      -> not visible  => SKIP silently (line 1246)
  - `Frozen(tid)`  (row tier)       -> not visible under `es_snapshot`
                                       => RETRY under `RegisteredSnapshotGuard::latest()`
                                       => still not visible => **ERROR** (line 1296)
  - `RemoteSkipped`(owner-reported) -> **SKIP** (line 1318)

request seq-03 §3 states a single rule — "Existing `tuple_payload_missing ->
RemoteSkipped` semantics for a sidecar tuple not visible under the read snapshot ...
if the row-tier tuple is also not visible, return the normal missing marker" — and
then says eligible `Frozen(row_tid)` local hits are converted "preserving the same
visibility rule". Applied to a local hit that rule is a behaviour change in either
direction, and the second-snapshot retry is unmentioned:

  (a) if the sidecar miss returns the missing marker, the candidate SKIPS where the
      control raises `EC_GENERATION_MISSING` (line 1296);
  (b) if the sidecar lookup runs only under `es_snapshot`, it misses in the window
      the control recovers from at lines 1269-1285, so the candidate skips or errors
      on a row the control returns.

Either way a covered local query diverges from the control on something other than
the covered mechanism, under exactly the concurrent same-identity replacement that
packet 003 checkpoint 5 must exercise.

